	url = {https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/B9780124201583000150},
	doi = {https://doi.org/10.1016/B978-0-12-420158-3.00015-0},
	abstract = {The efficient solving of scientific problems on supercomputers requires a sufficient amount of computational resources, and more often than not this involves performing floating-point calculations. Curiously, the fraction of silicon die area dedicated to supporting these computations is frequently quite modest compared to a modern processor's chip size. Providing specialized devices with an increased count of floating-point units may offer substantial speed advantage over conventional CPUs. Such accelerator devices need not necessarily be limited only to floating-point arithmetic, but may serve other purposes such as input/output management, cryptography, multimedia encoding and decoding, and signal processing. The chapter presents a historic overview of systems with integrated accelerators, starting with coprocessors and concluding with the discussion of modern graphics processing units—the prevalent accelerator type in high performance computing. An introduction to the heterogeneous system architecture that provides substantial advantages over discrete accelerator implementations is also presented.},
	booktitle = {High {Performance} {Computing}},
